Subject: [PATCH 10295/11362] Record what the selfparse A/B can and cannot
 decide

Operator directive, 2026-08-29, on seeing `mem=True` in an early cell: run-time
memorization by the live agent is ACCEPTABLE. So long as levels are being banked
and the challenge score improves, a world model that memorizes is only a defect
at TRAINING time, before a live challenge run is submitted. My earlier reading of
that flag as a bad signal was wrong.

It also bounds the experiment. The shards record holdout, visible_fit,
is_memorizing, memorization_scan and tool_loop_stats -- and NO level progress in
any form. The arm script imports arc_actions_to_progress solely to build windows
and never scores with it.

So this A/B selects on offline generalization, which is a proxy. If a memorizing
engine still banks levels, holdout is not the property driving the score, and an
A/B selecting on holdout could reject the better live method. A result here
answers "does the tool loop induce more generalizable engines", not "does it bank
more levels" -- and only the second decides whether to ship it.

The follow-up that would answer it is an actions-to-progress A/B
(REQ-ARC-WMTE-5720) over the same arms, counting actions to level-up with
memorization recorded as an observation rather than a penalty. Not started, and
named in the run README so it is not rediscovered later.

The run continues unchanged: a mid-run metric change would invalidate the banked
cells, and the generalization question is still worth answering.

